# This is a -bin package: openclaw is published to the npm registry with
# its dist/ already bundled (esbuild/Vite output invoked by upstream's own
# release pipeline), so no TypeScript build step is needed here at all --
# unlike app-backup/restic-browser (which vendors an npm dependency
# closure the same way but still runs `npm run build`), this ebuild's
# src_prepare only ever assembles files, never compiles anything.
#
# The published tarball's dist/*.js chunks dynamically `import()`/require()
# a much larger set of bare package specifiers than package.json's own
# "dependencies" field declares (many per-provider/per-channel plugin
# integrations are lazy-loaded), so simply placing dist/+openclaw.mjs on
# disk is not enough for full functionality: node_modules must be
# populated from the exact npm-shrinkwrap.json-locked closure below, the
# same way restic-browser assembles its frontend node_modules --
# individually fetched from registry.npmjs.org via SRC_URI and extracted
# directly into the right node_modules/<path> in src_prepare, with no
# `npm install` invocation (and therefore no network access) at any build
# phase.
#
# Two optional native-addon dependencies (@lydell/node-pty, used by
# terminal/pty-based skills, and sqlite-vec, used for local vector search)
# publish separate prebuilt linux-x64/linux-arm64 npm packages; only those
# two Linux variants are vendored below (gated on the amd64/arm64 KEYWORDS
# the same way net-im/claude-desktop-bin gates its .deb SRC_URI), darwin
# and win32 variants are dropped. Every other dependency in the closure is
# plain JS with no os/cpu constraint, so it applies to both KEYWORDS.
#
# Regenerate the arrays below (and files/${P}-third-party-licenses.txt,
# see the dodoc call in src_install) with:
#   scripts/generate-openclaw-npm-deps.py 2026.7.1.2 \
#     path/to/npm-shrinkwrap.json out.gen \
#     files/${P}-third-party-licenses.txt
# (npm-shrinkwrap.json is not committed to this overlay; re-download it
# from the published tarball at
# https://registry.npmjs.org/openclaw/-/openclaw-<upstream-version>.tgz
# for the version being bumped to.) The generator refuses to emit an entry
# for any dependency whose lockfile-recorded license does not pass plain,
# regex-based SPDX-like syntax validation (and explicitly rejects
# npm's own "UNKNOWN"/"UNLICENSED" non-answer placeholders by name), so
# pointing it at a fresh lockfile fails loudly instead of silently
# producing an ebuild with an inaccurate LICENSE. The third-party-licenses
# file it also writes is a deterministic, sorted "name@version: license"
# listing of every vendored package -- installed via dodoc below so it's
# independently verifiable without re-deriving it from the lockfile.

# python-any-r1.eclass does not (yet) support EAPI 9 (@SUPPORTED_EAPIS: 7
# 8; same constraint www-apps/open-webui-bin already hit and documented
# for python-single-r1.eclass); keep this at 8 until it does. Python here
# is a pure build-time tool (safe tarball extraction in src_unpack/
# src_prepare, see _openclaw_safe_extract below), never installed or run
# at runtime, so python-any-r1 (not python-single-r1/python-r1) is the
# correct eclass -- it depends on "any" installed, supported interpreter
# rather than pinning one into RDEPEND.
EAPI=8

PYTHON_COMPAT=( python3_{11..15} )

inherit python-any-r1

DESCRIPTION="Multi-channel AI gateway with extensible messaging integrations"
HOMEPAGE="https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw https://openclaw.org"

# Upstream's own version string uses a bare hyphen ("2026.7.1-2"), which
# PMS version syntax does not allow mid-version; MY_PV keeps the real
# upstream string for URL construction while PV/${P} stay Gentoo-legal.
MY_PV="2026.7.1-2"
